## The Numbers Speak for Themselves
As of today, there have been 714 food recalls this year alone, a staggering figure when compared to the total of 1072 recalls recorded in history. This means that 2026 is set to outpace previous years in terms of food safety issues. Out of this year's recalls, 181 were classified as Class I, indicating a reasonable probability that the use of the product could cause serious health consequences or death.

## Repeat Offenders
Certain brands have developed a concerning reputation. Notable among these are:

- Moonlight Packing Corporation
- Ghirardelli Chocolate Company
- Ambriola Co., Inc., The
- Everpress Juice
- Solina U.S. Holding
- Wellnov Supplements LLC
- Diva Fam Inc
- PS Seasoning & Spices, Inc.

Moonlight Packing Corporation leads the pack with a dismal score of 96 out of 100 (in this case, higher is worse), with 11 recalls to their name--all classified as Class I.

## Recent Offenses
The latest recalls highlight a mix of issues:

- **MID DOLCIARIA SRL** recalled their Cannoli products due to the use of unapproved food colors, E140 and E141. Food coloring may seem benign, but unapproved additives can pose unknown risks.

- **Greenworld Food Express (USA) LLC** pulled their Halawa Extra Pistachio due to potential Salmonella contamination, a dangerous bacterium that causes severe food poisoning.

- **Azuma Foods International Inc USA** faced a recall for their Tako Wasabi due to undeclared allergens, specifically fish.

- **Jonco Industries** had to recall White Cheddar Seasoning made with Salmonella-contaminated milk powder sourced from another supplier. This recall not only highlights the hazard posed by contaminated ingredients but also the ripple effect a single source can have across multiple products.

## What Can Consumers Do?
Stay informed. Check recall announcements regularly, especially for products from brands with a history of repeated offenses. Exercise caution with products that seem to have complex supply chains or ingredients prone to contaminants like dairy and seafood. Remember, it's your health on the line.
